    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Analysis of flow and pressure data from unfractured gas wells indicates that high velocity flow in the formation causes an additional pressure drop which is time independent and flowrate dependent. This high velocity flow effect is referred to in the literature as turbulent flow or non-Darcy flow.In the case of a fractured well, two flow regions can have non-Darcy effects: the formation and the fracture. Turbulent flow in the formation with laminar flow in the fracture has been researched and results published in the literature. The conclusion of these studies indicates that turbulent flow effects are significant if the well is intersected by a small fracture. For medium and large fractures, this formation effect is negligible, but for the case of non-Darcy flow in the fracture, the effects become more important.In this study, a well intersected by a fully penetrating finite conductivity vertical fracture is considered, with non-Darcy flow occurring only within the fracture. The petroleum literature has not analyzed this particular case in detail, and it is the intention of this study to fill that gap and lay the foundation for continuing research efforts in this area.This fractured is modeled by representing the flow regimes both in the fracture and in the formation by partial differential equations which can be coupled by source and sink terms. In the fracture, the non-Darcy effect is represented mathematically by a non-linear differential equation which includes a velocity term squared to account for the additional pressure drop. The fluid in the system is assumed to be slightly compressible and all other fluid and rock properties are assumed constant.Two types of flow boundary conditions at the wellbore were assumed: constant rate and constant pressure production. Semi-analytical solutions are presented for analyzing drawdown data for these conditions. In the case of constant rate production, a method is presented for analyzing pressure drawdown data to obtain the true fracture conductivity of the system. In the case of constant pressure production, the flow rate behavior is studied under different pressure drawdown conditions.Two finite difference models are presented for studying the boundary conditions given above. The results of these models match the semi-analytical solutions. The finite difference model for the constant rate case is further adapted to include the effects of real gas behavior at these high flowrates. This simulator also accounts for changing fracture conductivities due to pressure decline and increased closure pressure. Finally, this study investigates the effects of non-Darcy flow in buildup testing after constant rate production.",
